00:03:50.145 --> 00:03:55.785
being focused I think I like to make a
distinction between being distracted by

00:03:55.785 --> 00:03:58.454
things that don't relate to the language
you're learning because you have the

00:03:58.454 --> 00:04:04.035
distraction is related to the language
then that's different because I think

00:04:04.035 --> 00:04:11.445
one of the concepts in Goleman's book
about, uh, focus is that that, um, you

00:04:11.445 --> 00:04:16.485
know, to some extent we should be open
to new ideas, open to new stimulus and

00:04:16.485 --> 00:04:22.725
not constantly responding to the sort of,
if I understand the book again via the

00:04:22.725 --> 00:04:28.005
Persian, the sort of more basic instincts
of ours that make us afraid of this or

00:04:28.005 --> 00:04:29.655
nervous about that, or want to know about.

00:04:30.585 --> 00:04:35.205
And so that keeps us in a sort of
a established routine in a way we

00:04:35.205 --> 00:04:37.125
should be more open to new things.

00:04:37.125 --> 00:04:40.365
So the distractions
might be quite positive.

00:04:40.395 --> 00:04:47.985
For example, if my wife is watching a
Netflix series in Egyptian, which she

00:04:47.985 --> 00:04:51.765
was doing a couple of weeks ago, that's
a distraction and all of a sudden, wow, I

00:04:51.765 --> 00:04:54.105
want to do more Egyptian Arabic, Arabic.

00:04:54.105 --> 00:04:57.345
In other words, the distraction
can be a source of stimulus.

00:04:57.885 --> 00:05:04.365
And I think when we're we're language
learning, we're kind of, um, you know,

00:05:04.395 --> 00:05:08.505
vagabonds, we want to wander around
and sample some of this sample some of

00:05:08.505 --> 00:05:13.545
that, uh, it's not like we sort of sit
down and ok for the next hour I'm going

00:05:13.545 --> 00:05:16.875
to try to nail this vocabulary list.

00:05:17.205 --> 00:05:18.405
That's not very effective.

00:05:18.435 --> 00:05:25.215
If we allow ourselves to be distracted a
bit by um, that can bring a lot of reward

00:05:25.215 --> 00:05:29.115
and maybe makes our brains more open
because after all, when we're learning a

00:05:29.115 --> 00:05:32.655
language, we're trying to get this newest
thing, this new way of seeing things

00:05:32.655 --> 00:05:35.535
of expressing things into our brain.

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:38.595
And I think we need to be
flexible for that to happen.

00:05:38.625 --> 00:05:44.155
We need to be open to new ways of saying
things and this sort of flexibility

00:05:44.155 --> 00:05:47.880
in our attitude uh, I think it's a
big part of what makes some people

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.950
better language learners than others.

00:05:50.099 --> 00:05:54.270
So it may just be, and I
have no research to just...

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:59.340
to support this, but it may be that
a certain degree of openness, um,

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:04.260
ability to willingness to be distracted,
willingness to enjoy the distraction,

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:11.489
um, can be beneficial while at the same
time, if that is a distraction that

00:06:11.489 --> 00:06:15.960
takes you away from the language learning
tasks, then that's not a good thing.

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:21.210
And so in that sense, for example, I
